---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import Animations from '../../components/Animations.vue';
import CommentsSection from '../../components/CommentsSection.vue';
import BlogNavigation from '../../components/BlogNavigation.astro';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'pt' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const t = useTranslations(lang as any);

const content = {
  en: {
    title: "Dear Diary, What Is PSAUX Ransomware and How Does It Hijack a Linux Server? 🌸",
    date: "October 30, 2026",
    subtitle: "A developer diary entry by Jill",
    back: 'Back to Home',
    intro: "Most people think ransomware only happens on Windows workstations through phishing emails. But in the server world, automated malware hunts Linux VPS instances 24/7.",
    introDesc: "When the PSAUX ransomware campaign ripped through thousands of servers via control panel exploits last week, it left thousands of WordPress sites, databases, and local backups completely scrambled with a .psaux extension. Here is what PSAUX is, how Linux ransomware actually executes under the hood, why local backups fail, and how to build truly resilient disaster recovery.",
    bannerLabel: "Security Advisory & Threat Intelligence Analysis:",
    bannerUrl: "https://cybersecuritynews.com/cyberpanel-vulnerability-ransomware/",
    sections: [
      {
        heading: "1. The Wake-Up Call: Ransomware Isn't Just for Windows",
        paragraphs: [
          "For years, a common misconception among web developers was: *'Linux doesn't get viruses, and ransomware is something that only happens when an office worker clicks a suspicious PDF on Windows.'*",
          "Last week, the **PSAUX ransomware** campaign shattered that illusion for thousands of sysadmins and developers. By coupling automated vulnerability scanning with immediate root-level payload execution—as we saw in my previous entry on the <a href={`/${lang}/blog-cyberpanel-rce/`} class=\"inline-blog-link\">CyberPanel RCE vulnerability (CVE-2024-51567)</a>—attackers automated the compromise of entire servers in less than 30 seconds.",
          "Seeing production servers on <a href={`/${lang}/blog-droplets-vps/`} class=\"inline-blog-link\">cloud droplets and VPS instances</a> go dark simultaneously was a stark reminder: Linux servers are prime targets because that is where the crown jewels—production databases, source code, and client data—actually live."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "2. What is PSAUX Ransomware and How Does It Operate?",
        paragraphs: [
          "The name **PSAUX** comes from the infamous UNIX command `ps aux`, which lists all active running processes. The creators of this ransomware strain used this name both as their signature and as the file extension appended to every scrambled file (`.psaux`).",
          "Unlike desktop ransomware that waits for user interaction or crawls desktop folders, PSAUX is built specifically for **headless Linux servers**. It operates in a calculated sequence:",
          "**1. Process Termination:** The dropped shell script immediately runs `systemctl stop mysqld mariadb openlitespeed nginx apache2` to release file locks on database tables and web assets.",
          "**2. Competitive Malware Cleanup:** It inspects running processes and kills rival cryptocurrency miners and shell bots, ensuring it has maximum CPU to encrypt as fast as possible.",
          "**3. High-Speed Encryption:** It traverses `/home/`, `/var/www/`, and `/var/lib/mysql/`, using strong symmetric ciphers (AES or ChaCha20) to encrypt web files, media uploads, SQL dumps, and active database tables.",
          "**4. The Ransom Demand:** It writes `README_LOCKED.txt` in every web root and directory, demanding payments typically between 0.04 and 0.1 Bitcoin for the decryption key."
        ],
        codeLabel: "Anatomy of the PSAUX Execution Script (Decompiled Logic)",
        code: `#!/bin/bash
# Typical operational pattern of the PSAUX deployment script:

# 1. Kill services to release filesystem locks:
killall -9 mysqld mariadb httpd nginx lsphp 2>/dev/null
systemctl stop mysqld openlitespeed

# 2. Kill potential competing background miners:
pkill -f xmrig || pkill -f kdevtmpfsi

# 3. Encrypt target directories recursively:
# Uses embedded binary or OpenSSL with generated symmetric key
TARGETS=("/home" "/var/www" "/var/lib/mysql" "/backup")

for dir in "\${TARGETS[@]}"; do
    if [ -d "$dir" ]; then
        find "$dir" -type f ! -name "*.psaux" ! -name "README_LOCKED.txt" | while read -r file; do
            # Encrypt file and append .psaux extension
            encrypt_file "$file" && rm -f "$file"
        done
    fi
done

# 4. Drop ransom instructions:
echo "All your databases and web files have been locked by PSAUX." > /home/README_LOCKED.txt`
      },
      {
        heading: "3. The Tragedy of Local Backups: Why /backup Doesn't Save You",
        paragraphs: [
          "The most heartbreaking part of the PSAUX campaign wasn't just that live websites were encrypted—it was that many developers thought they were safe because they had automated daily backups configured on their server.",
          "Here is the brutal architectural reality: **If an attacker achieves `root` execution on your VPS, any backup stored on the same server or mounted as a writable volume is dead.**",
          "Default backup directories like `/backup`, `/var/backups`, or even secondary mounted block storage attached to `/mnt/backup` are easily found by `find / -type f` loops. The ransomware script encrypted the `.tar.gz` and `.sql` backup files right alongside the live databases.",
          "A backup that can be erased or encrypted by the same credentials running your web server isn't a backup—it is just a duplicate waiting to be destroyed."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "4. Building Real Disaster Recovery: The 3-2-1 Rule and Immutable Storage",
        paragraphs: [
          "Defending against ransomware on Linux doesn't mean hoping your web applications will never have a zero-day flaw. It means designing your infrastructure so that even if your server is completely erased by root, **you can restore everything in minutes**.",
          "**1. Offsite and Isolated Backups:** Your backup system must pull data *out* of your server, or push data to remote object storage (like AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, or Backblaze B2) using credentials that **only have append/write permissions**, never delete permissions.",
          "**2. Object Lock & Immutability:** Enable WORM (Write Once, Read Many) policies on your storage buckets. With Object Lock enabled, not even you (and certainly not the ransomware bot) can delete or overwrite a backup file for a specified retention window (e.g., 30 days).",
          "**3. Hypervisor Snapshots:** Enable daily snapshots at your cloud provider level (DigitalOcean, Linode, Hetzner). Cloud snapshots exist outside the virtual machine operating system, completely untouchable by a root shell inside the VPS."
        ],
        codeLabel: "Hardening Backups with Immutable Remote Storage",
        code: `# Best Practice: Using an unprivileged backup runner with S3 Object Lock
# The VPS credentials can ONLY upload, NEVER overwrite or delete:

# Daily database backup and compressed stream straight to offsite S3:
mysqldump --single-transaction -u backup_user -p"$DB_PASS" --all-databases | \\
  gzip -9 | \\
  aws s3 cp - s3://my-isolated-backups-bucket/$(date +%Y-%m-%d)-database.sql.gz \\
  --object-lock-mode COMPLIANCE \\
  --object-lock-retain-until-date $(date -d "+30 days" +%Y-%m-%d)

# Even if a root attacker runs 'rm -rf /' or encrypts the VPS disk,
# the S3 bucket refuses delete requests until the 30-day compliance window expires!`
      },
      {
        heading: "5. Lessons for Full-Stack Developers",
        paragraphs: [
          "Security isn't a single wall; it's a sequence of safety nets.",
          "This outbreak reminded me of my recent reflection on the <a href={`/${lang}/blog-php-cve-exploit/`} class=\"inline-blog-link\">PHP RCE incident on Forge</a>: vulnerabilities in third-party software *will* happen. We cannot prevent every upstream zero-day from being discovered.",
          "What we *can* control is our blast radius. By isolating our web server users, closing unnecessary management ports, and maintaining immutable, offsite backups, a catastrophic ransomware attack turns from a business-ending event into a simple 20-minute server rebuild from a snapshot."
        ]
      }
    ],
    remember: {
      heading: "Things I Want To Remember 🌸",
      items: [
        "**Linux ransomware is real and fast:** PSAUX proves that automated bots can stop services and encrypt thousands of databases in seconds.",
        "**Local backups are not disaster recovery:** Any backup file located on the same VPS or writable by root will be encrypted during an attack.",
        "**Object Lock is our best friend:** Using S3 or cloud storage with compliance retention prevents ransomware from destroying backups.",
        "**Hypervisor snapshots are immune to root shells:** Provider-level VPS snapshots exist outside the Linux OS and cannot be touched by in-guest malware.",
        "**Minimize the blast radius:** Never run public web applications or control panels with unrestricted root privileges."
